-- Refinement types, `cap no_panic` DIVISION SAFETY (2026-07-29). The accept
-- side of the pair with reject/t122. Both divisors carry a NON-LINEAR
-- refinement; the difference is whether the predicate actually entails
-- `d != 0`.
--
-- `v * v > 0` is exactly `v != 0` over the integers, and z3 decides it
-- instantly — but division_safety's own reflection refused any product without
-- a literal factor, and the arm that rejects an unreflectable refinement then
-- reported this program. `scale` pins the widened reflection.
--
-- `guarded` pins the other half: on the ELSE side of `if d == 0` the fact in
-- scope is `not (d == 0)`, which discharges the obligation on its own. The
-- syntactic path fallback used to drop negated conditions entirely, so this
-- was reported even though the guard makes the division unreachable with zero.
--
-- Exit 0 here means nothing on its own — a checker that accepted every
-- refinement it could not decide would also pass. reject/t122 is the twin that
-- gives this file its content: `v * v >= 0` is just as non-linear, just as
-- reflectable, and true of EVERY integer, so it must still be rejected.
mod NonlinearDivisor do
  needs IO.Console
  cap no_panic

  fn scale(d : {v : Int | v * v > 0}) : Int do
    10 / d
  end

  fn guarded(d : {v : Int | v * v > 0}) : Int do
    if d == 0 do 0 else 10 / d end
  end

  fn main(_cap_console : Cap(IO.Console)) do
    println(int_to_string(scale(2) + guarded(5)))
  end
end
